---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

import { stages } from "../lib/commands";

---

<Base
  title="How it works"
  description="The six stages of PolicyForge, from loading frameworks to asking questions, and the key command behind each."
>
  <main>
    <section class="page-hero">
      <div class="container">
        <p class="eyebrow">How it works</p>
        <h1>Six stages, from published frameworks to answers you can cite.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          The first three stages use no AI at all — they are careful data work, and most of
          the value. AI enters at stage four, and only to rework text it has been handed.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="container">
        {
          stages.map((s, i) => (
            <div class="split">
              <div class="split-head">
                <span class="step-n">Stage {i + 1}</span>
                <h2 style="font-size: 1.6rem;">{s.title}</h2>
                <p class="muted">{s.summary}</p>
              </div>
              <dl class="cmd-list">
                {s.cmds.filter(([name]) => s.key.includes(name)).map(([name, desc]) => (
                  <div class="cmd">
                    <dt>{name}</dt>
                    <dd>{desc}</dd>
                  </div>
                ))}
              </dl>
            </div>
          ))
        }
        <p class="table-note" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
          These are the key commands at each stage. All {stages.reduce((n, s) => n + s.cmds.length, 0)}
          are in the <a href="/docs/commands/">command reference</a>, including a loader for
          each framework.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section sunk">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="section-head">
          <p class="eyebrow">Three documents per topic</p>
          <h2>One merged list of requirements, written up three ways.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="grid">
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Policy</h3>
            <p>
              The commitments, in plain language for everyone in the organization. Short, and
              free of framework citations.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Standard</h3>
            <p>
              The full technical requirements, with every statement tagged to the controls it
              came from. This is what an assessor reads.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>Procedure</h3>
            <p>
              Step-by-step instructions for the people doing the work, still tagged to their
              sources.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="table-note">
          All three come from the same merged list, so they cannot drift out of step with
          each other. Markdown is the only format written; Confluence pages are converted from
          it, so there is one version of the truth.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>
